best","best","inaccuracy","inaccuracy","great","best","excellent"],"moments":[{"ply":22,"name":"11…Nh5","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"11…Nh5! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Moving away uncovers the bishop on g7, which now hits the knight on c3.","The next best move, 11…Bf8, would drop Black's winning chances from 49% to 38% (+1.30).","11…Nh5 stops Nb5 and uncovers an attack on the knight on c3.","White was threatening Nb5, winning a pawn, picking it up with 12.Nxd6. Nh5 takes care of it.","White answers 12.Be3.","Engine line: 12.Be3 Nc7 13.a4 b6 14.Qb3 Ba6 (+0.05)."]},{"ply":44,"name":"22…Rxe3","cls":"brilliant","color":"b","headline":"22…Rxe3!! A real sacrifice: Black gives up material and the engine still rates it best.","points":["Puts the rook on e3 where it can be taken.","White can take, but after 23.fxe3 Be5 24.Bf3 Nf6 the engine rates it −2.85.","Takes the bishop on e3.","22…Rxe3 takes the bishop on e3.","White answers 23.fxe3, winning the exchange.","After 23.fxe3 Be5 24.Bf3 Nf6 25.Qc1 Ra8, Black is two pawns down (−2.85)."],"why":[{"title":"The idea","text":"22…Rxe3!! offers the rook on e3. Taking it gives Black an attack worth more than the rook."},{"title":"What Black gives up","text":"22…Rxe3 puts the rook on e3 where it can be taken by the f2 pawn. That's a whole rook on offer."},{"title":"What it does instead","text":"It takes the bishop on e3."},{"title":"If White takes the rook","text":"23.fxe3 is met by 23…Nf6, bringing the passive knight into play. Black is still two pawns down, but the attack is worth more: Black is clearly better (−1.95). This is White's best try."},{"title":"Without the sacrifice","text":"The best move without the sacrifice, 22…Ra8, is clearly weaker (−1.51 against −2.85). White answers 23.Bf3, preparing Bd2 and Bc3. Black would still be clearly better, but far less than after 22…Rxe3."}]},{"ply":68,"name":"34…Qc4","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"34…Qc4! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["The next best move, 34…Qa7, would drop Black's winning chances from 74% to 51% (−0.08).","34…Qc4 threatens …Qxb3, winning the queen, and stops Qxa2.","It threatens …Qxb3, winning the queen, so White has to spend the next move on it.","White was threatening Qxa2, winning a rook and a pawn. Qc4 takes care of it.","The engine's plan continues with …c3 and …Bh6: this move is the first step.","White answers 35.Qxc4, taking the queen on c4, leaving Black with doubled c-pawns."]},{"ply":72,"name":"36…Bxe5","cls":"miss","color":"b","headline":"36…Bxe5 misses 36…Nd2, which wins material. Black's winning chances go from 78% to 50%.","points":["White answers 37.Be4: moving away uncovers the rook on f1, which now hits the f7 pawn.","After Be4, White wins 1 point of material within 4 moves.","Better was 36…Nd2 (−3.50), which attacks the rook on f1."],"better":"36…Nd2 wins a bishop for a pawn, picking it up with 37…Nxe4. After 37.Be4 Nxe4 38.Rxf7 Kg8 39.Rc7 Bxe5 40.R7xc2 Rxc2 41.Rxc2 Nc3, the rooks are traded and Black comes out two pawns up (−3.50)."},{"ply":73,"name":"37.Be4","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"37.Be4! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Moving away uncovers the rook on f1, which now hits the f7 pawn.","The next best move, 37.Bg2, would drop White's winning chances from 50% to 13% (−5.26).","37.Be4 wins a pawn, picking it up with 38.Bxc2, and threatens Rxf7+, winning a pawn.","Sets up a sequence and wins a pawn once the captures settle, picking it up with 38.Bxc2.","It threatens Rxf7+, winning a pawn, so Black has to spend the next move on it.","Black answers 37…f5, attacking the bishop on e4, which is undefended."]},{"ply":78,"name":"39…Ne3","cls":"great","color":"b","headline":"39…Ne3! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["The knight forks the rook and the rook: both can't be saved at once.","Shores up the f5 pawn, which was under as much attack as it had defence (2 against 1).","The next best move, 39…Nb6, would drop Black's winning chances from 50% to 36% (+1.51).","39…Ne3 wins the exchange, picking it up with 40…Nxc2, and threatens …Nxf1, winning a rook.","Sets up a sequence and wins the exchange once the captures settle, picking it up with 40…Nxc2.","It threatens …Nxf1, winning a rook, so White has to spend the next move on it."]},{"ply":107,"name":"54.Rd3","cls":"great","color":"w","headline":"54.Rd3! The only good move here: every alternative is clearly worse.","points":["Shores up the e3 pawn, which was under as much attack as it had defence (1 against 1).","The next best move, 54.Bf5, would drop White's winning chances from 47% to 31% (−2.22).","54.Rd3 threatens Rxa3, winning a rook for a pawn.","It threatens Rxa3, winning a rook for a pawn, so Black has to spend the next move on it.","Black answers 54…Ra4, joining the attack on the king.","Engine line: 54…Ra4 55.Bc2 Rc4 56.Bb3 Rc7 57.Bd1 (−0.15)."]}],"opening":{"name":"English Opening: Great Snake Variation","eco":"A10","points":["Both sides followed opening theory up to 11.Nd2: 21 moves in all, 11 by White and 10 by Black.","Theory runs out after 11.Nd2: too few games between strong players reached this position to go further."]}}